8:    """
9:    Recolor the cell regions of a lattice so perimeter regions become red and interior regions become green.
10:
11:    Input structure:
12:    - The input has exactly two colors: one lattice color and one cell-region color.
13:    - The lattice color fills the whole outer border and separates rectangular 4-connected cell regions.
14:    - The cell regions form a complete rectangular array with at least three rows and three columns.
15:
16:    Rule:
17:    - Preserve every lattice pixel.
18:    - For each cell region, recolor it red if its region-row or region-column is on the outer edge of the array.
19:    - Recolor all strictly interior cell regions green.
20:
21:    Key insight:
22:    - The black cells are not independent pixels; they are the holes of a larger lattice, and their array position determines the fill color.
23:    """
24:    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
25:    assert g.ndim == 2, "expected a 2D grid"
26:    height, width = map(int, g.shape)
27:
28:    colors = set(map(int, np.unique(g)))
29:    assert len(colors) == 2, "expected exactly two input colors"
30:
31:    border_size_with_corners_counted_twice = 2 * height + 2 * width
32:    lattice_colors = [
33:        color
34:        for color in colors
35:        if int(
36:            (g[0, :] == color).sum()
37:            + (g[-1, :] == color).sum()
38:            + (g[:, 0] == color).sum()
39:            + (g[:, -1] == color).sum()
40:        )
41:        == border_size_with_corners_counted_twice
